  "title": "Distroless images: smaller attack surface",
  "content": "The workaround gets copy-pasted forward long after the original reason is forgotten. The same skills that mistake shows up across production codebases often enough to be a pattern, not bad luck. This post covers what comes after the happy path.\n\nA container image is a filesystem, a process entrypoint, and a set of metadata. Hardening it means shrinking what's in that filesystem and what that process can do. The usual starting point is a base image like `ubuntu:22.04` or `node:18`. Those images are built for general use, so they include a shell, package managers, and debugging tools. For a build environment that's helpful. For a runtime container it's a liability: every extra binary is another potential entry point for an attacker, and every extra package is another CVE to track.\n\nThe problem this post solves is that teams often treat image hardening as a one-time cleanup — swap to Alpine, run a scanner, call it done — and then the next base image update reintroduces a shell or a vulnerable library. Hardening has to be a repeatable pipeline step, not a heroic effort. Below is a practical sequence: choose a minimal base, move to distroless where it fits, run a scanner that actually blocks bad builds, and verify the result with the same tools an attacker would use.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\nYou need a container runtime (Docker or Podman), a registry you can push to, and a CI system that can run a scanner. The examples use Docker and GitHub Actions, but the same steps work with GitLab CI, Jenkins, or any runner that can execute shell commands.\n\nYou also need to know two things about your application: which dynamic libraries it links against at runtime, and whether it needs a shell for its entrypoint script. If the answer to the second is yes, distroless will require a small refactor — more on that later.\n\n## Step 1: Measure the image you have before changing anything\n\nBefore you pick a base image, get a baseline. Two numbers matter: the number of packages installed and the number of known vulnerabilities. You can get both without a scanner for the package count:\n\n```bash\ndocker run --rm --entrypoint sh your-image:latest -c 'dpkg -l | wc -l'\n```\n\nFor vulnerabilities, use a scanner. Trivy is a widely used open-source option that works on local images and in CI:\n\n```bash\ntrivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL your-image:latest\n```\n\nRecord the counts. This baseline is what you'll compare against after each change. Without it, you can't tell whether a switch to Alpine actually helped or just moved the CVEs around.\n\nA common trap at this stage: scanning only the `latest` tag. If your deployment uses a digest or a version tag, scan that exact reference. Tags move; digests don't.\n\n## Step 2: Pick a minimal base image that matches your runtime\n\nThe first instinct is Alpine. Alpine uses musl libc instead of glibc, and that difference matters. Native Node.js modules, Python wheels with C extensions, and Go binaries built with cgo can all behave differently on musl. Some prebuilt wheels won't load at all. The failure mode is usually a runtime error like `Error loading shared library libc.musl-x86_64.so.1` or a segfault that doesn't reproduce on your glibc-based dev machine.\n\nIf your dependencies are pure (no native extensions), Alpine is a fine choice and the image is small. If you have native dependencies, a better fit is a slim variant of the same distribution you build on — for example `debian:bookworm-slim` or `python:3.12-slim`. Slim variants strip documentation, man pages, and compilers but keep glibc, so native modules keep working.\n\nA decision table helps here:\n\n| Base | libc | Typical use | Watch out for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `alpine:3.20` | musl | Pure-language apps, static Go binaries | Native modules, DNS resolution differences |\n| `debian:bookworm-slim` | glibc | Apps with native extensions | Still includes apt and a shell |\n| `gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12` | none (static) | Go, Rust static binaries | No shell, no package manager |\n| `gcr.io/distroless/base-debian12` | glibc | Apps needing libc but not a shell | Debugging is harder |\n\nDistroless images, maintained by Google, contain only the runtime dependencies your app needs — no shell, no package manager, no `apt`. The `static` variant has no libc at all; the `base` variant includes glibc. There are language-specific variants (`java`, `python3`, `nodejs`) that bundle the runtime but still omit the shell.\n\n## Step 3: Build a multi-stage Dockerfile that discards the build toolchain\n\nThe most reliable way to get a small runtime image is to build in one stage and copy only the artifacts into the final stage. Here's a Go example that produces a static binary and runs it on distroless:\n\n```dockerfile\n# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1\nFROM golang:1.22-bookworm AS build\nWORKDIR /src\nCOPY go.mod go.sum ./\nRUN go mod download\nCOPY . .\nRUN CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=linux go build -trimpath -ldflags='-s -w' -o /out/app ./cmd/app\n\nFROM gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot\nCOPY --from=build /out/app /app\nUSER nonroot:nonroot\nENTRYPOINT [\"/app\"]\n```\n\nThree things in that final stage matter. `CGO_ENABLED=0` produces a static binary so it doesn't need libc. `-ldflags='-s -w'` strips debug symbols, which reduces size (and slightly reduces the information an attacker gets from the binary). `USER nonroot:nonroot` runs as a non-root user — the distroless `nonroot` tag includes that user, and running as root inside a container is a privilege escalation risk if the container is compromised.\n\nFor Python, the pattern is similar but the final stage uses `gcr.io/distroless/python3-debian12`. You copy your virtualenv or site-packages into the image. One gotcha: distroless Python expects your app to be importable; you can't run `pip install` at runtime because there's no shell or package manager. Install dependencies in the build stage and copy them.\n\nFor Node.js, use `gcr.io/distroless/nodejs20-debian12`. The entrypoint is `node`, so your `CMD` should be the path to your script, not `npm start` — there's no npm in the image.\n\n## Step 4: Fix the entrypoint-script problem\n\nDistroless images have no shell. Any `ENTRYPOINT` or `CMD` that starts with `sh -c` or uses shell syntax will fail with an error like `exec: \"/bin/sh\": stat /bin/sh: no such file or directory`. This is the most common reason a distroless migration stalls.\n\nThe fix is to make the container run a single executable directly. If you currently have a start script that sets environment variables, waits for a database, or runs migrations, move that logic into your application or into an init container. For example, instead of:\n\n```dockerfile\nCMD [\"sh\", \"-c\", \"node server.js\"]\n```\n\nuse:\n\n```dockerfile\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\n\nIf you need environment variable substitution, do it at deploy time (your orchestrator can set env vars) rather than in a shell. If you need to wait for a dependency, use your platform's health-check or init-container mechanism — Kubernetes `initContainers` or a Compose `depends_on` with a healthcheck — rather than a shell loop.\n\nA second common trap: some base images set `ENTRYPOINT` to a shell script. When you switch to distroless, that entrypoint disappears. Always check the base image's Dockerfile or documentation for its default `ENTRYPOINT` and `CMD`, and override them explicitly.\n\n## Step 5: Add a vulnerability scanner that fails the build\n\nScanning is only useful if it blocks. A scanner that prints a report but exits zero gives you a dashboard nobody reads. Configure your scanner to fail on HIGH and CRITICAL severities, and set a policy for what counts as acceptable.\n\nWith Trivy in GitHub Actions:\n\n```yaml\nname: build-and-scan\non: [push]\njobs:\n  scan:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - name: Build image\n        run: docker build -t app:${{ github.sha }} .\n      - name: Scan image\n        uses: aquasecurity/trivy-action@0.24.0\n        with:\n          image-ref: app:${{ github.sha }}\n          format: table\n          exit-code: '1'\n          ignore-unfixed: true\n          severity: HIGH,CRITICAL\n```\n\n`ignore-unfixed: true` is a pragmatic default: it skips vulnerabilities that have no available fix, so your build doesn't fail on something you can't remediate. The trade-off is that you're accepting those vulnerabilities. Review the ignore list periodically and document why each is accepted.\n\nA second scanner worth running is Grype, which uses a different vulnerability database and can catch things Trivy misses. Running two scanners is not paranoia — their databases update on different schedules and have different coverage. If you only run one, pick one and keep its database updated; a stale database is worse than no scanner because it gives false confidence.\n\n## Step 6: Verify the runtime, not just the build\n\nA clean scan at build time doesn't guarantee a clean runtime. The image can be modified after the scan (by a compromised registry or a malicious layer), and the running container can have a different filesystem than the image if volumes are mounted.\n\nVerify three things after deployment:\n\n1. **The running image digest matches what you scanned.** In Kubernetes, `kubectl get pod <pod> -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[0].imageID}'` shows the digest. Compare it to the digest you scanned.\n2. **The process is not running as root.** `kubectl exec <pod> -- id` will fail on distroless because there's no `id` binary, but you can check the pod spec's `securityContext.runAsNonRoot: true` and `runAsUser`. If the pod spec doesn't set it, the image's `USER` directive may still apply, but explicit is better.\n3. **No unexpected open ports or capabilities.** Use `kubectl exec` with a debug container (`kubectl debug`) if you need to inspect a distroless pod; don't add a shell to the production image just for debugging.\n\nA useful verification step is to run the image locally with a read-only root filesystem and see if it still starts:\n\n```bash\ndocker run --rm --read-only --tmpfs /tmp app:latest\n```\n\nIf the app writes to disk outside `/tmp`, it will fail. That failure is informative: it tells you where the app expects to write and whether that should be a mounted volume instead. Many hardened deployments run with `readOnlyRootFilesystem: true` in Kubernetes, so catching this early avoids a production surprise.\n\n## Edge cases and errors\n\n**DNS resolution on Alpine.** musl's DNS resolver behaves differently from glibc's, particularly with search domains and `ndots`. A service that resolves fine on Debian can fail on Alpine with `no such host`. If you see that, check `/etc/resolv.conf` and consider switching to a glibc-based slim image.\n\n**CA certificates.** Distroless `static` images include CA certificates, but if you build `FROM scratch` you must copy them yourself: `COPY --from=build /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt /etc/ssl/certs/`. Without them, outbound HTTPS calls fail with `x509: certificate signed by unknown authority`.\n\n**Timezones.** Distroless images include tzdata in the `base` and language variants, but `static` does not. If your app formats timestamps in a local timezone, either use UTC everywhere or copy tzdata into the image.\n\n**Debugging without a shell.** `kubectl exec -it <pod> -- sh` will fail on distroless. Use `kubectl debug` to attach an ephemeral container with a shell and the same process namespace. This is the intended workflow, and it's safer than shipping a shell in the production image.\n\n**Scanner [false positives on](/stop-false-positives-in-ci-scanning-now/) distroless.** Distroless images sometimes report CVEs for packages that are present in the image metadata but not actually used at runtime. Check whether the vulnerable binary is reachable. If it isn't, document the exception rather than switching back to a larger base image just to make the scanner quiet.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**Does distroless mean I can't use a package manager in production?**\nCorrect. Distroless images have no `apt`, `apk`, or `yum`. All dependencies must be installed in the build stage and copied into the final image. This is the point: it removes a common attacker tool and reduces image size. If you need to install something at runtime, distroless is the wrong base for that container.\n\n**Is Alpine more secure than Debian slim?**\nNot inherently. Alpine has a smaller default package set, which reduces attack surface, but musl compatibility issues can force you to add packages or work around native modules. The more important factor is how often you rebuild and rescan. A Debian slim image rebuilt weekly with a scanner in CI is usually in better shape than an Alpine image that hasn't been rebuilt in six months.\n\n**How do I handle vulnerabilities with no fix?**\nMost scanners let you ignore unfixed vulnerabilities or add them to an allowlist with an expiry date. The policy should be explicit: accept the risk, document why, and set a reminder to re-check when a fix is released. Don't silently ignore them; that's how a known CVE stays in production for years.\n\n**Can I use distroless with a language that needs a JIT compiler?**\nYes, for the languages that have distroless variants. The `java` variant includes a JRE, and the `nodejs` variant includes Node.js. The constraint is the same: no shell, no package manager. If your app spawns subprocesses that expect a shell, those will fail; rewrite them to call executables directly.\n\n## The next 30 minutes\n\nOpen your main service's Dockerfile and change the final stage to a distroless base that matches your runtime — `gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot` for a static Go binary, or the appropriate language variant otherwise. Then run `trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL <your-image>` against both the old and new image and compare the counts. If the new image fails to start, check the entrypoint first: a `sh -c` command is the most likely cause.\n\n## Official documentation\n\n- [Kubernetes documentation](https://kubernetes.io/docs/home/)\n- [Docker documentation](https://docs.docker.com/)\n- [The Rust Programming Language](https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/)\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** November 2026\n",
